 succeeded in 403ms:
10:- **Batch re-pricing:** the first waves measure real per-item cost (wall time, failure modes); the wave size is re-priced from that evidence at Condense and reported to HQ — 5 is a starting price, not a law.
12:- **Empty-pool form (promoted 2026-11-15, Cycle 010 Condense — candidate filed in Cycle 009's Condense; promotion condition was a second empty firing demonstrating the same shape, and Wave 8 is that firing; clause wording derives from the Cycle-009 candidate):** a wave with zero owed first-attempt items is a lawful cycle, not a skipped one. Its product is the finding form: (1) a pool census priced from the QUEUE recreation section, the catalogue register (TECHNIQUES.md + INDEX.md), the intake directory, and a binding artifact census (per-directory scores.md + codex/ return presence), plus board hand-offs where intake is pending; (2) the cycle-level guard firings — codex-observe in its cycle-level form, jev-observe-exit, jev-plan-exit, codex-condense — fired unconditionally, with per-item/per-build calls (codex-plan-review · jev-prewave · the five-lens scoring wave) printed NOT-DEMANDED-BY-FORM with their reasons; (3) the filings (HQ.json entry · verdict-history no-verdict line · lane heartbeat beat); (4) upstream intake state priced in the open (what is built, what stands unregistered/unadopted, which job owns the next act) — an empty pool here is never printed as an empty lane upstream, and the next wave's Observe re-censuses fresh rather than inheriting the finding. No item is invented, no practice re-attempt is misrouted, and no verdict is counterfeited for an unbuilt wave. First demonstration: Cycle 009 Wave 7 (2026-11-14). Second demonstration + this promotion: Cycle 010 Wave 8 (2026-11-15).
39:4. **Self-verify before Codex** (§5 legs 1–3); a build that fails its own swap test does not spend a Codex wave. Self-verify files the A12 executable receipts (§5 leg 3b) in the build-record's first draft; a build-record missing receipts does not spend the prewave call either.
49:3b. **Pre-wave Jev gate:** `guards/jev-prewave.md` judges the build record after legs 1–3 and before the wave (leg 4): FIRE-WAVE spends the wave; HOLD returns the build to Execute with its classification and the wave moves to its next item. **Receipt readiness check (Amendment A12, 2026-11-18 — performed by the builder BEFORE the payload is assembled):** every A11 checkpoint in the build-record carries its executable receipt — the harness command or run, the CONFIG expression evaluated, and the raw result from the build under review; for a CONFIG timing swap the receipt shows the changed evaluation instant. A checkpoint with a claim but no receipt returns the record to the builder before the call is spent. **Receipt-index reconciliation (Amendment A13, 2026-11-18):** the build-record carries a RECEIPT INDEX enumerating (a) every guards.md command applicable to the item and (b) every A11 assertion for the item; before the payload is assembled the builder reconciles the index against the applicable guard commands, the item's planned A11 assertions, and — for a completion or re-attempt — the assertion sets established in the filed prior harnesses and their amendments, and, for census receipts, against the build's expected CONFIG keys (nested/array keys included). Every index line carries its executable receipt or an explicit NOT-DEMANDED line whose disposition cites the governing plan or guard; a missing expected line, an unsupported exclusion, or a line with neither returns the record to the builder. A receipt is valid only if it carries the evidence its assertion requires: for each CONFIG-derived input, the CONFIG expression and its evaluated value; for each check, the raw observed result. A populated receipt lacking that evidence returns the record to the builder on the same form. This check is a readiness check for the mandatory call, not a gate on it: the call still fires at its step on every cycle, and a cycle in which it did not fire prints INCOMPLETE under the standing firing ruling. The deliverable-class judgment for this job IS the five-lens scoring wave (leg 4, defined in `guards/codex-scoring-wave.md`) — no separate jev-verify file exists, by design: the wave's per-lens verdicts + leg 5's mechanical consolidation are the deliverable gate, and a second Jev over the same deliverable would duplicate them.
87:## §9 — Condense
95:## Judgment-layer presence evaluation (standing — owner's 100% order, 2026-11-14)
99:**Ruling addendum (2026-11-14 ~20:55 EST — owner's judgment-call ruling):** arming = firing. This job's Jev/Codex steps are non-skippable and fire unconditionally in its next live cycle, raw responses filed in the run record; verdicts are advisory — applied, or set aside with the miss recorded in Condense + the call-file amendment it teaches. The founding print above reads superseded where it phrases any call as gated, conditional, or UNBUILT-blocked; presence from the next cycle prices whether the prescribed firings HAPPENED (a cycle closing without one is INCOMPLETE).
105:**Schedule basis is a required Plan field.** The §9 pattern rule fired this cycle: two techniques' timing lenses independently priced the same defect class — a recreation whose schedule is silently re-based from the segment's measured timeline (T64 lens-timing −16: calendar entrances at 0.4/1.4 s vs measured ~4/5 s · T66 lens-timing-interim1 −18: opening state mismatching the measured window). Standing requirement, binding on every Plan from Wave 12 onward: each item's window-scope line in the wave RUN.md names its **schedule basis** — `segment-anchored` (the build's phases sit at their measured segment times), `t=0 allocation` (the technique's lifecycle is re-based to the build's own zero, with the re-basing named as an allocation), or `two-act` (a measured-window act plus a labeled allocation-model act) — and the item's build-record carries the same line. A Plan whose window-scope line lacks the schedule basis is incomplete in the same way a Plan lacking its swap values is (jev-plan-exit prices it). Landed by Cycle 013's Condense under the pattern rule; HQ may amend or retire it, and until then it is operative instruction, not candidacy.
108:Landed by Cycle 023's first Replan item, the named consumer of Cycle 022 §9's five outstanding amendments. Each clause is operative instruction, binding from Wave 21 onward.
114:6. **Parameterization depth (A9.6 — pattern promotion, §9 rule: the reusability finding recurred across T139 + T140 in Cycle 023, both lens-minimum at 78).** Every subject-shaping value is CONFIG data, not a code literal: vehicle/geometry branch data, background-layer geometry and growth/opacity coefficients (a background layer's coefficients are subject-shaping when a lens can price them against the subject — the T140 dust classification is resolved this way), and plate tile data. The config-census leg (A6) prices literals of these classes as HITs from Wave 21 onward; guard-config-census.md's classification text is amended in the same landing's guard sweep or carries the not-yet-amended note in the cycle record that first applies it.
